# Līdza guide for {{.Name}}

Written by `lidza new`. One source of truth for every agent working here;
`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and `GEMINI.md` point at this file.

## What this is

A Līdza app is one Go binary. In production it serves the API under `/api`
and the built frontend for every other path. In development, `lidza dev`
runs the same binary with the frontend proxied from its dev server, so the
app is always at one address: http://127.0.0.1:3000.

## Layout

```
{{.Name}}/
├── main.go          entrypoint: lidza.Run(...). Do not edit.
├── routes.go        API handlers. Add routes here (or in packages it calls).
├── lidza.json       project config: name, frontend template, dev server, dist
├── go.mod           module {{.Name}}, requires {{.Module}}
{{- if .Dist}}
├── package.json     frontend ({{.Template}}); npm scripts dev, build, check
├── src/             frontend source
│   ├── api.ts       every call to /api goes through here
│   ├── router.tsx   client-side routes
│   └── pages/       one file per page
├── {{.Dist}}/            frontend build output, embedded into the binary. Never edit.
{{- end}}
├── docs/lidza-guide.md   this file
└── .lidza/          dev build artifacts (gitignored)
```

## Commands

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `lidza dev` | starts the frontend dev server{{if .DevURL}} ({{.DevURL}}){{end}}, builds and runs the app on http://127.0.0.1:3000, rebuilds on Go changes. `npm install` runs automatically when `node_modules` is missing. |
| `lidza build` | builds the frontend into `{{if .Dist}}{{.Dist}}/{{else}}(none){{end}}`, then compiles `bin/{{.Name}}`: one binary, no Node at runtime. |
| `lidza version` | prints the framework version. |
| `go test ./...` | Go tests. |
{{- if .Dist}}
| `npm run check` | frontend type check (`tsc --noEmit`). |
{{- end}}

Coming with the framework's roadmap: `lidza check --json` (one diagnostics
stream for Go, Rust and frontend errors), `lidza mcp` (route map, schema and
logs over MCP), and the generated `@lidza/client`.

## Rules

1. Go owns `/api`. The frontend never defines an API route and never proxies
   one; it calls `/api/...` on the same origin.
2. Add an API route in `routes.go` with a `net/http` pattern
   (`"GET /api/v1/things/{id}"`) and reply with `router.JSON`. Keep handlers
   under `/api/v1/`.
{{- if .Dist}}
3. Frontend calls to the API live in `src/api.ts` only. When the generated
   client (`@lidza/client`) arrives, that file is replaced; nothing else
   should need to change.
4. Never edit `{{.Dist}}/`; `lidza build` regenerates it.
{{- end}}
5. Do not run the frontend dev server or the Go binary by hand; `lidza dev`
   starts both and keeps them in sync.
6. Keep the app stateless: no global mutable maps, no in-memory sessions.
   State goes to Postgres or Redis.

## Adding an API route

```go
// routes.go
r.HandleFunc("GET /api/v1/time", func(w http.ResponseWriter, req *http.Request) {
	router.JSON(w, http.StatusOK, map[string]string{"now": time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339)})
})
```

Save; `lidza dev` rebuilds and restarts the binary. Check with
`curl -s http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/v1/time`.
{{- if .Dist}}

## Adding a page

1. Create `src/pages/Things.tsx` exporting a component.
2. In `src/router.tsx`, declare `createRoute({ getParentRoute: () => rootRoute, path: '/things', component: Things })` and add it to `addChildren([...])`.
3. Fetch data with `useQuery` and a function from `src/api.ts`.
{{- end}}

## Environment the binary reads

| Variable | Meaning | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `LIDZA_ADDR` | listen address | `127.0.0.1:3000` |
| `LIDZA_MODE` | `dev` proxies the frontend instead of serving the embedded build | unset (production) |
| `LIDZA_FRONTEND_URL` | dev server to proxy to; set by `lidza dev` | |
